    "evidence_text": "To account for the ordering of the\n\ncategories, learners are penalized according to how far from the true class their predictions are.\n\nAn important benefit of multitask\n\nBy jointly learning correlated tasks, the amount of usable data increases.\n\nspans.\n\nWe use the high-quality datasets the challenge organizers released.\n\nFeature representation We report results using two\n\ndifferent feature sets.\n\nSimple compositional functions like average were shown to be robust and efficient in previous work  .\n\nreader to  , whose open implementation we used to extract them.\n\nThe models To evaluate the multitask learning approach, we compared it with several other models.\n\nto dimension 50.\n\nWe used the Root Mean Square Propagation optimization method.\n\nWe used dropout for regularizing the network.\n\nDuring training,\n\nThe upper part of the Table summarizes the performance of the baselines.\n\nSeveral observations can be made from the table.\n\nThis entails that the system makes use of the available resources efficiently and improves the state-of-the-art performance.\n\nOn the other hand, the baseline systems were retrained on\n\nthe union of the train and development sets.\n\nWe also highlight the positive effect of the additional features that previous research proposed.\n\nAdding the features both in the baselines and in the biLSTM-based architectures improves the INLINEFORM1 scores by several points.\n\nLastly, we compare the performance of the baseline systems with the performance of the state-of-the-art system of  .\n\nSimilar observations can be drawn from Figure FIGREF10 that presents the INLINEFORM0 scores.\n\nThis opens several avenues for future research.",
